window.docContents[73].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-rigidity":{"contents":"The key step towards surjectivity is a rigidity lemma: an automorphism g fixing 0\nand all the basis vectors is the identity. The point is that the coordinates of a\nvertex can be read off from distances, which automorphisms preserve. Since the graph\ndistance in the Hamming graph is the Hamming distance (hypercubeHamming_edist_eq),\nevery automorphism preserves the Hamming distance, and (if it fixes 0) the Hamming\nweight hammingNorm, i.e. the number of true coordinates, which is the Hamming\ndistance to 0.\n\nlemma hammingDist_aut {n : ℕ} (g : hypercubeHamming n ≃g hypercubeHamming n)\n    (x y : Fin n → Bool) : hammingDist (g x) (g y) = hammingDist x y := by\n  have h := g.edist_eq x y\n  rw [hypercubeHamming_edist_eq, hypercubeHamming_edist_eq] at h\n  exact_mod_cast h\n\n\nlemma hammingNorm_aut {n : ℕ} {g : hypercubeHamming n ≃g hypercubeHamming n}\n    (hg : g 0 = 0) (x : Fin n → Bool) : hammingNorm (g x) = hammingNorm x := by\n  rw [← hammingDist_zero_right, ← hg, hammingDist_aut, hammingDist_zero_right]\n\n\nAdding a basis vector Pi.single i true flips the i-th coordinate; if that\ncoordinate was true this decreases the weight by one.\n\nlemma hammingNorm_add_single {n : ℕ} {x : Fin n → Bool} {i : Fin n}\n    (h : x i = true) :\n    hammingNorm (x + Pi.single i true) + 1 = hammingNorm x := by\n  have hset : ({k | (x + Pi.single i true : Fin n → Bool) k ≠ 0} : Finset _) =\n      ({k | x k ≠ 0} : Finset _).erase i := by\n    ext j\n    by_cases hj : j = i\n    · simp [hj, h, Bool.zero_eq_false, Bool.add_eq_xor]\n    · simp [hj]\n  rw [hammingNorm, hset, Finset.card_erase_add_one (by simp [h, Bool.zero_eq_false])]\n  rfl\n\n\nThe vertex x + eₖ differs from x only in coordinate k, so its weight is one\nless than that of x if x k = true, and one more otherwise. Since the distance\nfrom x to eₖ is the weight of x + eₖ, we see that x k = true exactly when x\nis closer to eₖ than to 0.\n\nlemma apply_eq_true_iff_hammingDist_lt {n : ℕ} (x : Fin n → Bool) (k : Fin n) :\n    x k = true ↔ hammingDist x (Pi.single k true) < hammingDist x 0 := by\n  rw [hammingDist_zero_right, hammingDist_eq_hammingNorm, boolVec_neg]\n  cases hk : x k\n  · have h : (x + Pi.single k true : Fin n → Bool) k = true := by\n      simp [hk, Bool.add_eq_xor]\n    have := hammingNorm_add_single h\n    rw [add_assoc, boolVec_add_self, add_zero] at this\n    grind\n  · have := hammingNorm_add_single hk\n    grind\n\n\nAn automorphism g fixing 0 and eₖ preserves the distances from any x to 0\nand to eₖ, so by the previous lemma g x and x agree in coordinate k.\n\nlemma aut_eq_id_of_fix_zero_and_singles {n : ℕ}\n    {g : hypercubeHamming n ≃g hypercubeHamming n} (h0 : g 0 = 0)\n    (h1 : ∀ i, g (Pi.single i true) = Pi.single i true) (x : Fin n → Bool) :\n    g x = x := by\n  funext k\n  rw [Bool.eq_iff_iff, apply_eq_true_iff_hammingDist_lt,\n    apply_eq_true_iff_hammingDist_lt, ← h1 k, ← h0, hammingDist_aut, hammingDist_aut,\n    h1 k, h0]\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Algebraic Graph Theory\u0009The Automorphism Group of the Hypercube*","header":"12.6.4. Rigidity","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-rigidity"},"/Appendix/#section-chapter-6-cliques":{"contents":"* mycielskianProj: projection from the vertex set of mμ[G] to G mapping both\n  left and right copies to the original vertex.* openNeighborhoodSet: the open neighbourhood of a set s, i.e. the union of the\n  neighbourhoods of its vertices.* openNeighborhoodFinset: the Finset version, for a locally finite graph: the\n  vertices adjacent to at least one vertex of s.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Appendix\u0009Definitions","header":"14.2.5. Chapter 6: Cliques","id":"/Appendix/#section-chapter-6-cliques"},"/Cliques/#section-triangles-and-independent-sets":{"contents":"In a triangle-free graph the neighbourhood of any vertex is an independent set, since\ntwo adjacent neighbours of v would form a triangle with v.\n\n#check isIndepSet_neighborSet_of_triangleFree\n\n\nHence in a finite triangle-free graph every vertex has degree at most ⍺ G.\n\nlemma degree_le_indepNum_of_triangleFree [Finite V] [G.LocallyFinite]\n    (h : G.CliqueFree 3) (v : V) : G.degree v ≤ ⍺ G := by\n  rw [← card_neighborFinset_eq_degree]\n  exact IsIndepSet.card_le_indepNum\n    (by simpa using G.isIndepSet_neighborSet_of_triangleFree h v)\n\n\nSo in a triangle-free graph with no large independent sets every vertex has small\ndegree. Applying this to both a graph and its complement is the idea behind the\nsimplest case of Ramsey's theorem. The Ramsey number R(3, 3) is the least n\nsuch that every graph on n vertices contains a triangle or an independent set of\nsize 3. Mathlib has no graph Ramsey numbers, but we can still prove the classical\nvalue R(3, 3) = 6.\n\nThe 5-cycle shows that R(3, 3) > 5: it is triangle-free and has no independent\nset of size 3. As explained above, we rewrite IndepSetFree in terms of the\ncomplement before calling decide.\n\nexample : C[5].CliqueFree 3 ∧ C[5].IndepSetFree 3 :=\n  ⟨by decide, by rw [← cliqueFree_compl]; decide⟩\n\n\nexample : ⍺ C[5] = 2 := by\n  have h2 : C[5].IsNIndepSet 2 {0, 2} := by decide\n  exact h2.indepNum_eq_of_indepSetFree (by rw [← cliqueFree_compl]; decide)\n\n\nThe upper bound R(3, 3) ≤ 6 is left as an exercise: applying\ndegree_le_indepNum_of_triangleFree to both G and Gᶜ does most of the work.\n\nThe 5-cycle also shows that the product of the clique and independence numbers\ncan be smaller than the number of vertices: ω C[5] * ⍺ C[5] = 4 < 5.\nContrast this with the bound |V| ≤ χ G * ⍺ G that we will prove in the\nnext chapter. On the other hand the sum satisfies ω G + ⍺ G ≤ |V| + 1 for every\nfinite graph, since a clique and an independent set share at most one vertex (see the\nexercises).\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Cliques\u0009Independent Sets","header":"6.6.1. Triangles and Independent Sets","id":"/Cliques/#section-triangles-and-independent-sets"},"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-more-decidable-adjacency-instances":{"contents":"We give two more instances of decidable adjacency, for the disjoint unions of graphs\nintroduced in Sum And Sigma Graphs; an instance for the line graph\nis left to the exercises.\n\nRecall Σg G the SimpleGraph.sigma disjoint union of an indexed family of graphs.\n\n#source SimpleGraph.sigma -- protected def sigma (G : (i : ι) → SimpleGraph (V i)) :\n                          --     SimpleGraph (Σ i, V i) where\n                          --   Adj := fun ⟨i, u⟩ ⟨j, v⟩ =>\n                          --            ∃ h : i = j, (G i).Adj u (h ▸ v)\n\n\nWe can give an instance of DecidableRel (Σg G).Adj for SimpleGraph.sigma,\n(assuming the index type has decidable equality and that each of the graphs in the\nfamily has decidable adjacency). Here we have to do a case split on i = j (using\nthe decidable equality of the index type).\n\ninstance {ι : Type*} {V : ι → Type*} (G : (i : ι) → SimpleGraph (V i))\n    [DecidableEq ι] [∀ i, DecidableRel (G i).Adj] : DecidableRel (Σg G).Adj :=\n  fun ⟨i, u⟩ ⟨j, v⟩ =>\n    if h : i = j then\n  -- when the indices agree, adjacency reduces to adjacency in the single graph G i\n      decidable_of_iff ((G i).Adj u (h ▸ v)) (by subst h; simp)\n    else\n  -- vertices in different parts are never adjacent\n      isFalse (by simp [h])\n\n\nRecall SimpleGraph.sum, the disjoint union of two graphs G ⊕g H. This is missing\nan instance of DecidableRel (G ⊕g H).Adj in Mathlib.\n\ninstance {V W : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph V) (H : SimpleGraph W) [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n    [DecidableRel H.Adj] : DecidableRel (G ⊕g H).Adj := fun u v =>\n  match u, v with\n  | Sum.inl u', Sum.inl v' => inferInstanceAs (Decidable (G.Adj u' v'))\n  | Sum.inr u', Sum.inr v' => inferInstanceAs (Decidable (H.Adj u' v'))\n  -- There is something slightly odd about the definition of SimpleGraph.sum\n  -- Non-adjacency of vertices from different parts is defined using false : Bool,\n  -- rather than False : Prop, hence the need for Decidable false below.\n  | Sum.inl _, Sum.inr _ => inferInstanceAs (Decidable false)\n  | Sum.inr _, Sum.inl _ => inferInstanceAs (Decidable false)\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Decidability, Finiteness and Degrees\u0009Decidability\u0009Deciding Adjacency","header":"4.1.4.2. More Decidable Adjacency Instances*","id":"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-more-decidable-adjacency-instances"},"/Theorems/#section-exercises-theorems":{"contents":"1. Show that a graph with an isolated vertex has no perfect matching.\n\nexample (v : V) (hv : ∀ w, ¬ G.Adj v w) :\n    ¬ ∃ M : G.Subgraph, M.IsPerfectMatching := by\n  sorry\n\n\n2. Use Hall's theorem to show that the complete bipartite graph K[n, n] has a\nperfect matching. Hint: Sum.swap maps every set of vertices injectively into its\nneighbourhood.\n\nexample (n : ℕ) : ∃ M : K[n, n].Subgraph, M.IsPerfectMatching := by\n  sorry\n\n\n3. Use Hall's theorem to show that the infinite path pathNat₁ has a perfect\nmatching. Hint: n ↦ n + 1 maps every finite set of vertices injectively into its\nneighbourhood. What happens for infinite sets?\n\nexample : ∃ M : pathNat₁.Subgraph, M.IsPerfectMatching := by\n  sorry\n\n\n4. Deduce the two-sided version of Hall's theorem: if every vertex lies in p₁ or\np₂, and Hall's condition holds for the subsets of each part, then G has a perfect\nmatching. Why is the hypothesis hV needed? Hint: reduce to\nexists_isPerfectMatching_of_forall_ncard_le by splitting s into s ∩ p₁ and\ns ∩ p₂, whose neighbourhoods are disjoint.\n\nexample [G.LocallyFinite] {p₁ p₂ : Set V} (h₁ : G.IsBipartiteWith p₁ p₂)\n    (hV : p₁ ∪ p₂ = Set.univ)\n    (h₂ : ∀ s ⊆ p₁, s.ncard ≤ (⋃ x ∈ s, G.neighborSet x).ncard)\n    (h₃ : ∀ s ⊆ p₂, s.ncard ≤ (⋃ x ∈ s, G.neighborSet x).ncard) :\n    ∃ M : G.Subgraph, M.IsPerfectMatching := by\n  sorry\n\n\n5. Show that the complete graph on a finite type V has a perfect matching iff\nFintype.card V is even.\n\nexample [Fintype V] :\n    (∃ M : (⊤ : SimpleGraph V).Subgraph, M.IsPerfectMatching) ↔\n      Even (Fintype.card V) := by\n  sorry\n\n\n6. Show that every vertex has at most two neighbours in the symmetric difference of\ntwo perfect matchings.\n\nexample {M M' : G.Subgraph} (hM : M.IsPerfectMatching) (hM' : M'.IsPerfectMatching)\n    (v : V) : ((symmDiff M.spanningCoe M'.spanningCoe).neighborSet v).ncard ≤ 2 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n7. Show that the Turán graph turanGraph n r is r-colourable for r > 0. (So by\nTurán's theorem every Turán-maximal (r + 1)-clique-free graph is r-colourable.)\n\nexample (n r : ℕ) (hr : 0 < r) : (turanGraph n r).Colorable r := by\n  sorry\n\n\n8. Use Mantel's theorem to bound the number of edges of the Mycielskian of a\ntriangle-free graph.\n\nexample [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] (h : G.CliqueFree 3) :\n    #mμ[G].edgeFinset ≤ (2 * Fintype.card V + 1) ^ 2 / 4 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n9. Show that a bipartite graph on n vertices has at most n ^ 2 / 4 edges.\n\nexample [Fintype V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] (h : G.IsBipartite) :\n    #G.edgeFinset ≤ Fintype.card V ^ 2 / 4 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n10. Show that the bound in the Andrásfai–Erdős–Sós theorem cannot be improved for\nr = 2: the blow-up C[5][t] has n = 5t vertices and is triangle-free with\nminimum degree 2t = 2n / 5, but it is not bipartite.\n\nexample (t : ℕ) :\n    C[5][t + 1].CliqueFree 3 ∧ ¬ C[5][t + 1].Colorable 2 ∧\n      ∀ v, C[5][t + 1].degree v = 2 * (t + 1) := by\n  sorry\n\n\n11. Use Zykov's theorem to show that a 4-clique-free graph on 6 vertices has at\nmost as many triangles as the octahedron completeEquipartiteGraph 3 2. Hint:\nMathlib's completeEquipartiteGraph.turanGraph is an isomorphism from\ncompleteEquipartiteGraph r t to turanGraph (r * t) r.\n\nexample [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] (hV : Fintype.card V = 6)\n    (h : G.CliqueFree 4) :\n    #(G.cliqueFinset 3) ≤ #((completeEquipartiteGraph 3 2).cliqueFinset 3) := by\n  sorry\n\n\n12. Show that a graph has as many 2-cliques as edges. Hint: Sym2.toFinset sends\nthe edge s(x, y) to {x, y}.\n\nlemma card_cliqueFinset_two' [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n    #(G.cliqueFinset 2) = #G.edgeFinset := by\n  sorry\n\n\n13. Deduce the edge bound of Turán's theorem, CliqueFree.card_edgeFinset_le, from\nZykov's theorem with s = 2.\n\nexample [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] {r : ℕ}\n    (h : G.CliqueFree (r + 1)) : #G.edgeFinset ≤ turanNumber (Fintype.card V) r := by\n  sorry\n\n\n14. Restate Mantel's theorem as a formula for an extremal number.\n\nexample (n : ℕ) : extremalNumber n K[3] = n ^ 2 / 4 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n15. Deduce that a graph on n vertices with more than n ^ 2 / 4 edges contains a\ntriangle.\n\nexample [Fintype V] [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n    (h : Fintype.card V ^ 2 / 4 < #G.edgeFinset) : K[3] ⊑ G := by\n  sorry\n\n\n16. Show that a graph on n vertices whose triangles are edge-disjoint has at most\nn.choose 2 / 3 triangles.\n\nexample [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n    (h : G.EdgeDisjointTriangles) :\n    3 * #(G.cliqueFinset 3) ≤ (Fintype.card V).choose 2 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n17. Let G have no triangles and no 5-cycles. Use the count of 5-cycles in mμ[G]\nfrom Counting 5 Cycles In Mycielskians to bound the number of\nedges that killCopies deletes from mμ[G] to make it free of 5-cycles.\n\nexample [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] (h3 : G.CliqueFree 3)\n    (h5 : C[5].Free G) :\n    2 * #mμ[G].edgeFinset ≤ 2 * #(mμ[G].killCopies C[5]).edgeFinset +\n      ∑ u, ∑ v ∈ G.neighborFinset u, G.degree u * G.degree v := by\n  sorry\n\n\n18. Show that the grid hasse ℤ □ hasse ℤ has exactly one end, as in\nEnds. Hint: if K lies in the square [-N, N] × [-N, N] then the\nrows and columns outside the square avoid K. Use ComponentCompl.mem_of_hom with\nboxProdLeft and boxProdRight to show that every infinite component of G - K\ncontains (N + 1, N + 1), and apply subsingleton_end_of_common_vertex.\n\nexample : Nat.card (hasse ℤ □ hasse ℤ).end = 1 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n1. Show that a graph with an isolated vertex has no perfect matching.example (v : V) (hv : ∀ w, ¬ G.Adj v w) :\n    ¬ ∃ M : G.Subgraph, M.IsPerfectMatching := by\n  rintro ⟨M, hM⟩\n  obtain ⟨w, hw, -⟩ := Subgraph.isPerfectMatching_iff.1 hM v\n  exact hv w hw.adj_sub\n2. Use Hall's theorem to show that the complete bipartite graph K[n, n] has a\nperfect matching. Hint: Sum.swap maps every set of vertices injectively into its\nneighbourhood.example (n : ℕ) : ∃ M : K[n, n].Subgraph, M.IsPerfectMatching := by\n  refine exists_isPerfectMatching_of_forall_ncard_le\n    (IsBipartiteWith.completeBipartiteGraph _ _) fun s => ?_\n  refine Set.ncard_le_ncard_of_injOn Sum.swap (fun x hx => ?_)\n    Sum.swap_leftInverse.injective.injOn (Set.toFinite _)\n  exact Set.mem_biUnion hx (by cases x <;> simp)\n3. Use Hall's theorem to show that the infinite path pathNat₁ has a perfect\nmatching. Hint: n ↦ n + 1 maps every finite set of vertices injectively into its\nneighbourhood. What happens for infinite sets?example : ∃ M : pathNat₁.Subgraph, M.IsPerfectMatching := by\n  refine exists_isPerfectMatching_of_forall_ncard_le\n    (p₁ := {n | Even n}) (p₂ := {n | Odd n}) ?_ fun s => ?_\n  · refine ⟨Set.disjoint_left.2 fun n h1 h2 => Nat.not_even_iff_odd.2 h2 h1, ?_⟩\n    rintro v w (rfl | rfl) <;>\n      simp [Nat.even_add_one, ← Nat.not_even_iff_odd, em, em']\n  · by_cases hs : s.Finite\n    · refine Set.ncard_le_ncard_of_injOn (· + 1) (fun x hx => ?_)\n        (add_left_injective 1).injOn\n        (hs.biUnion fun x _ => (pathNat₁.neighborSet x).toFinite)\n      exact Set.mem_biUnion hx (by simp)\n    · rw [Set.Infinite.ncard hs]\n      exact Nat.zero_le _\n4. Deduce the two-sided version of Hall's theorem: if every vertex lies in p₁ or\np₂, and Hall's condition holds for the subsets of each part, then G has a perfect\nmatching. Why is the hypothesis hV needed? Hint: reduce to\nexists_isPerfectMatching_of_forall_ncard_le by splitting s into s ∩ p₁ and\ns ∩ p₂, whose neighbourhoods are disjoint.example [G.LocallyFinite] {p₁ p₂ : Set V} (h₁ : G.IsBipartiteWith p₁ p₂)\n    (hV : p₁ ∪ p₂ = Set.univ)\n    (h₂ : ∀ s ⊆ p₁, s.ncard ≤ (⋃ x ∈ s, G.neighborSet x).ncard)\n    (h₃ : ∀ s ⊆ p₂, s.ncard ≤ (⋃ x ∈ s, G.neighborSet x).ncard) :\n    ∃ M : G.Subgraph, M.IsPerfectMatching := by\n  -- hV is needed: a vertex in neither part has no neighbours, so no perfect\n  -- matching covers it, although the one-sided conditions can still hold.\n  refine exists_isPerfectMatching_of_forall_ncard_le h₁ fun s => ?_\n  rcases s.finite_or_infinite with hs | hs\n  · -- split s into its parts in p₁ and p₂, whose neighbourhoods are disjoint\n    let N : Set V → Set V := fun t => ⋃ x ∈ t, G.neighborSet x\n    have hfin {t : Set V} (ht : t ⊆ s) : (N t).Finite :=\n      (hs.subset ht).biUnion fun x _ => (G.neighborSet x).toFinite\n    have hsplit : s = (s ∩ p₁) ∪ (s ∩ p₂) := by\n      rw [← Set.inter_union_distrib_left, hV, Set.inter_univ]\n    have hN₁ : N (s ∩ p₁) ⊆ p₂ :=\n      Set.iUnion₂_subset fun x hx => isBipartiteWith_neighborSet_subset h₁ hx.2\n    have hN₂ : N (s ∩ p₂) ⊆ p₁ :=\n      Set.iUnion₂_subset fun x hx => isBipartiteWith_neighborSet_subset h₁.symm hx.2\n    calc s.ncard = (s ∩ p₁).ncard + (s ∩ p₂).ncard := by\n          conv_lhs => rw [hsplit]\n          exact Set.ncard_union_eq\n            (h₁.disjoint.mono Set.inter_subset_right Set.inter_subset_right)\n            (hs.subset Set.inter_subset_left) (hs.subset Set.inter_subset_left)\n      _ ≤ (N (s ∩ p₁)).ncard + (N (s ∩ p₂)).ncard :=\n          add_le_add (h₂ _ Set.inter_subset_right) (h₃ _ Set.inter_subset_right)\n      _ = (N s).ncard := by\n          rw [← Set.ncard_union_eq (h₁.disjoint.symm.mono hN₁ hN₂)\n            (hfin Set.inter_subset_left) (hfin Set.inter_subset_left)]\n          conv_rhs => rw [hsplit]\n          simp only [N, Set.biUnion_union]\n  · simp [hs.ncard]\n5. Show that the complete graph on a finite type V has a perfect matching iff\nFintype.card V is even.example [Fintype V] :\n    (∃ M : (⊤ : SimpleGraph V).Subgraph, M.IsPerfectMatching) ↔\n      Even (Fintype.card V) := by\n  refine ⟨fun ⟨M, hM⟩ => hM.even_card, fun h => ?_⟩\n  have hK : (⊤ : SimpleGraph V).IsClique Set.univ := isClique_univ.2 rfl\n  obtain ⟨M, hverts, hM⟩ := (hK.even_iff_exists_isMatching Set.finite_univ).1\n    (by rwa [Set.ncard_univ, Nat.card_eq_fintype_card])\n  exact ⟨M, hM, Subgraph.isSpanning_iff.2 hverts⟩\n6. Show that every vertex has at most two neighbours in the symmetric difference of\ntwo perfect matchings.example {M M' : G.Subgraph} (hM : M.IsPerfectMatching) (hM' : M'.IsPerfectMatching)\n    (v : V) : ((symmDiff M.spanningCoe M'.spanningCoe).neighborSet v).ncard ≤ 2 := by\n  rcases ((symmDiff M.spanningCoe M'.spanningCoe).neighborSet v).eq_empty_or_nonempty\n    with h | h\n  · simp [h]\n  · exact (hM.symmDiff_isCycles hM' h).le\n7. Show that the Turán graph turanGraph n r is r-colourable for r > 0. (So by\nTurán's theorem every Turán-maximal (r + 1)-clique-free graph is r-colourable.)example (n r : ℕ) (hr : 0 < r) : (turanGraph n r).Colorable r :=\n  ⟨Coloring.mk (fun v => ⟨v % r, Nat.mod_lt _ hr⟩) fun h => by\n    simpa [Fin.ext_iff] using turanGraph_adj.1 h⟩\n8. Use Mantel's theorem to bound the number of edges of the Mycielskian of a\ntriangle-free graph.example [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] (h : G.CliqueFree 3) :\n    #mμ[G].edgeFinset ≤ (2 * Fintype.card V + 1) ^ 2 / 4 := by\n  have := (CliqueFree.mycielskian h).card_edgeFinset_le (r := 2)\n  rwa [turanNumber_two, Fintype.card_option, Fintype.card_sum, ← two_mul]\n    at this\n9. Show that a bipartite graph on n vertices has at most n ^ 2 / 4 edges.example [Fintype V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] (h : G.IsBipartite) :\n    #G.edgeFinset ≤ Fintype.card V ^ 2 / 4 := by\n  rw [← turanNumber_two]\n  exact (Colorable.cliqueFree h (by norm_num)).card_edgeFinset_le\n10. Show that the bound in the Andrásfai–Erdős–Sós theorem cannot be improved for\nr = 2: the blow-up C[5][t] has n = 5t vertices and is triangle-free with\nminimum degree 2t = 2n / 5, but it is not bipartite.example (t : ℕ) :\n    C[5][t + 1].CliqueFree 3 ∧ ¬ C[5][t + 1].Colorable 2 ∧\n      ∀ v, C[5][t + 1].degree v = 2 * (t + 1) := by\n  refine ⟨(blowUpGraph_cliqueFree_iff _ _ fun _ => 0).2 (by decide), fun h => ?_, ?_⟩\n  · have h5 := ((blowUpGraph_colorable_iff _ fun _ => 0).1 h).chromaticNumber_le\n    rw [chromaticNumber_cycleGraph_of_odd 5 (by norm_num) (by decide)] at h5\n    exact absurd h5 (by decide)\n  · rintro ⟨v, i⟩\n    rw [degree_blowUpN, cycleGraph_degree_three_le, mul_comm]\n11. Use Zykov's theorem to show that a 4-clique-free graph on 6 vertices has at\nmost as many triangles as the octahedron completeEquipartiteGraph 3 2. Hint:\nMathlib's completeEquipartiteGraph.turanGraph is an isomorphism from\ncompleteEquipartiteGraph r t to turanGraph (r * t) r.example [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] (hV : Fintype.card V = 6)\n    (h : G.CliqueFree 4) :\n    #(G.cliqueFinset 3) ≤ #((completeEquipartiteGraph 3 2).cliqueFinset 3) := by\n  have e := completeEquipartiteGraph.turanGraph (r := 3) (t := 2)\n  have := h.card_cliqueFinset_le (r := 3) 3\n  rwa [hV, ← e.card_cliqueFinset_eq] at this\n12. Show that a graph has as many 2-cliques as edges. Hint: Sym2.toFinset sends\nthe edge s(x, y) to {x, y}.A graph has as many 2-cliques as edges./-- A graph has as many 2-cliques as edges. -/\nlemma card_cliqueFinset_two [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n    #(G.cliqueFinset 2) = #G.edgeFinset := by\n  symm\n  refine card_bij (fun e _ => e.toFinset) (fun e he => ?_) (fun e _ e' _ h => ?_)\n    (fun t ht => ?_)\n  · induction e using Sym2.ind with\n    | _ x y =>\n      rw [mem_edgeFinset, mem_edgeSet] at he\n      rw [Sym2.toFinset_mk_eq, mem_cliqueFinset_iff]\n      exact ⟨by rw [coe_pair]; exact isClique_pair.2 fun _ => he, card_pair he.ne⟩\n  · exact Sym2.ext fun x => by rw [← Sym2.mem_toFinset, h, Sym2.mem_toFinset]\n  · rw [mem_cliqueFinset_iff] at ht\n    obtain ⟨x, y, hxy, rfl⟩ := card_eq_two.1 ht.card_eq\n    refine ⟨s(x, y), ?_, Sym2.toFinset_mk_eq⟩\n    rw [mem_edgeFinset, mem_edgeSet]\n    exact ht.isClique (by simp) (by simp) hxy\n13. Deduce the edge bound of Turán's theorem, CliqueFree.card_edgeFinset_le, from\nZykov's theorem with s = 2.example [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] {r : ℕ}\n    (h : G.CliqueFree (r + 1)) : #G.edgeFinset ≤ turanNumber (Fintype.card V) r := by\n  have := h.card_cliqueFinset_le 2\n  rwa [card_cliqueFinset_two, card_cliqueFinset_two] at this\n14. Restate Mantel's theorem as a formula for an extremal number.example (n : ℕ) : extremalNumber n K[3] = n ^ 2 / 4 := by\n  rw [extremalNumber_top, Fintype.card_fin]\n  exact turanNumber_two\n15. Deduce that a graph on n vertices with more than n ^ 2 / 4 edges contains a\ntriangle.example [Fintype V] [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n    (h : Fintype.card V ^ 2 / 4 < #G.edgeFinset) : K[3] ⊑ G := by\n  have h3 : extremalNumber (Fintype.card V) K[3] = Fintype.card V ^ 2 / 4 := by\n    rw [extremalNumber_top, Fintype.card_fin]\n    exact turanNumber_two\n  exact IsContained.of_extremalNumber_lt_card_edgeFinset (h3 ▸ h)\n16. Show that a graph on n vertices whose triangles are edge-disjoint has at most\nn.choose 2 / 3 triangles.example [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n    (h : G.EdgeDisjointTriangles) :\n    3 * #(G.cliqueFinset 3) ≤ (Fintype.card V).choose 2 :=\n  h.card_edgeFinset_le.trans card_edgeFinset_le_card_choose_two\n17. Let G have no triangles and no 5-cycles. Use the count of 5-cycles in mμ[G]\nfrom Counting 5 Cycles In Mycielskians to bound the number of\nedges that killCopies deletes from mμ[G] to make it free of 5-cycles.example [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] (h3 : G.CliqueFree 3)\n    (h5 : C[5].Free G) :\n    2 * #mμ[G].edgeFinset ≤ 2 * #(mμ[G].killCopies C[5]).edgeFinset +\n      ∑ u, ∑ v ∈ G.neighborFinset u, G.degree u * G.degree v := by\n  rw [← copyCount_mycielskian_cycleGraph_five h3 h5]\n  have := le_card_edgeFinset_killCopies_add_copyCount (G := mμ[G]) (H := C[5])\n  grind\n18. Show that the grid hasse ℤ □ hasse ℤ has exactly one end, as in\nEnds. Hint: if K lies in the square [-N, N] × [-N, N] then the\nrows and columns outside the square avoid K. Use ComponentCompl.mem_of_hom with\nboxProdLeft and boxProdRight to show that every infinite component of G - K\ncontains (N + 1, N + 1), and apply subsingleton_end_of_common_vertex.example : Nat.card (hasse ℤ □ hasse ℤ).end = 1 := by\n  have hadj (n : ℤ) : (hasse ℤ).Adj n (n + 1) :=\n    hasse_adj.2 (.inl (Order.covBy_add_one n))\n  have hP : (hasse ℤ).Preconnected := hasse_preconnected_of_succ ℤ\n  rw [Nat.card_eq_one_iff_unique]\n  refine ⟨subsingleton_end_of_common_vertex fun K => ?_, ?_⟩\n  · obtain ⟨N, hN⟩ :=\n      (K.finite_toSet.image fun v : ℤ × ℤ => max |v.1| |v.2|).bddAbove\n    -- the column {a} × ℤ and the row ℤ × {b} avoid K when N < |a|, N < |b|\n    let col (a : ℤ) := (boxProdRight (hasse ℤ) (hasse ℤ) a).toHom\n    let row (b : ℤ) := (boxProdLeft (hasse ℤ) (hasse ℤ) b).toHom\n    have hcol (a : ℤ) (ha : N < |a|) (n : ℤ) : col a n ∉ K := fun hK =>\n      ha.not_ge ((le_max_left _ _).trans (hN ⟨_, hK, rfl⟩))\n    have hrow (b : ℤ) (hb : N < |b|) (n : ℤ) : row b n ∉ K := fun hK =>\n      hb.not_ge ((le_max_right _ _).trans (hN ⟨_, hK, rfl⟩))\n    have hN1 : N < |N + 1| := (lt_add_one N).trans_le (le_abs_self _)\n    -- every infinite component of G - K contains (N + 1, N + 1)\n    refine ⟨(N + 1, N + 1), fun C hC => ?_⟩\n    obtain ⟨⟨a, b⟩, habC, hbox⟩ :=\n      (hC.sdiff ((Set.finite_Icc (-N) N).prod (Set.finite_Icc (-N) N))).nonempty\n    simp only [Set.mem_prod, Set.mem_Icc, ← abs_le, not_and_or, not_le] at hbox\n    rcases hbox with ha | hb\n    · have := ComponentCompl.mem_of_hom hP (col a) (hcol a ha) (u := b) habC\n      exact ComponentCompl.mem_of_hom hP (row _) (hrow _ hN1) (u := a) (this _) _\n    · have := ComponentCompl.mem_of_hom hP (row b) (hrow b hb) (u := a) habC\n      exact ComponentCompl.mem_of_hom hP (col _) (hcol _ hN1) (u := b) (this _) _\n  · have (n : ℤ) : Fintype ((hasse ℤ).neighborSet n) :=\n      Fintype.ofFinset {n - 1, n + 1} fun m => by\n        simp [hasse_adj, Order.covBy_iff_add_one_eq]; grind\n    have : Fact (hasse ℤ □ hasse ℤ).Preconnected := ⟨hP.boxProd hP⟩\n    exact (nonempty_ends_of_infinite _).to_subtype\n\n\nend SimpleGraph\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Theorems","header":"13.6. Exercises","id":"/Theorems/#section-exercises-theorems"}});